n divMax-Sampled CASP15 E.2 Additional LRCP Precision Visualizations E.3 Hump E.4 Hump Result on Full CASP15 E.5 α \alpha Control Knob License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2607.22314v1 [cs.LG] 24 Jul 2026 Evolution-Aware MSA Reasoning for Subsampling via Factor Graphs Zhangzhi Xiong 1,∗ Minzhang Li 1,∗ Haotian Yu 1,∗ Sixian Shen 1 Kexin Zhang 1 Mingrui Li 1 Jie Zheng 1,† Kewei Tu 1,† Jingyi Yu 1,† 1 School of Information Science and Technology, ShanghaiTech University xiongzhzh2023@shanghaitech.edu.cn ∗ Equal contribution. † Corresponding authors Abstract Multiple Sequence Alignments (MSAs) provide protein language models with explicit evolutionary context, but their large depth makes subsampling unavoidable under limited token budgets. Existing strategies, including random selection, identity-based filtering, and diversity-driven sampling, are effective heuristics, yet provide limited control over the evolutionary signals retained in the subset. In this work, we recast MSA subsampling as an explicit optimization problem, where key evolutionary measures, including query identity and diversity, are treated as controllable objectives. Building on this view, we introduce AP-REASONER, an Affinity-Propagation-based factor-graph approach. With evolution-aware unary factors, exemplar-consistency factors, and two control knobs, AP-REASONER performs factor-graph reasoning through message passing to infer a fixed-budget MSA subset. Experiments on long-range contact prediction and conformational ensemble prediction show that AP-REASONER outperforms baseline subsamplers on structure-sensitive downstream tasks and enables controllable recovery of alternative protein conformations. These results highlight the value of modeling MSA subsampling as a controllable optimization problem, where factor-graph reasoning offers an effective alternative to heuristic selection. 1 Introduction Evolution has profoundly shaped and diversified the biological language of proteins over eons [ 3 , 5 ] . Across this vast temporal scale, the sequence identity preserved by shared ancestry couples with the diversity introduced by stochastic mutations, insertions, and deletions, collectively defining a constrained and richly populated sequence space [ 31 , 28 , 17 ] . This sequence space is, in essence, a functional landscape that deeply encodes the physics of protein folding and biological constraints [ 8 , 19 , 26 ] . Consequently, precisely and rationally modeling it has emerged as the pivotal key for diverse downstream applications, including contact and structure prediction [ 41 , 19 , 4 , 1 , 48 ] , dynamic conformational analysis [ 6 , 49 ] , and drug discovery [ 1 , 11 , 10 ] . The transition from evolutionary theory to computational modeling has birthed two divergent paradigms for “presenting” evolutionary signals to protein language models (PLMs) [ 7 , 37 ] . Single-sequence models [ 40 , 26 , 14 , 9 , 32 , 27 ] relegate these signals to an implicit prior; by training on vast unaligned databases, they leverage generative objectives to internalize conservation patterns and structural constraints directly within their neural parameters [ 46 , 39 ] . In contrast, MSA-based models [ 38 , 19 , 1 , 16 , 50 ] adopt a more explicit approach, employing Multiple Sequence Alignments (MSAs) [ 44 , 29 , 45 ] as a discrete representation of the evolutionary manifold to provide a stronger inductive bias [ 38 , 2 ] . However, this explicit dependency is stifled by a severe computational trade-off: due to the quadratic complexity of attention mechanisms, such models can only process a narrow context of a few hundred sequences [ 38 , 19 ] —a mere fraction of the rich evolutionary information available in genomic databases [ 45 , 29 ] . Figure 1 : The left part illustrates two main MSA indicators for evolution: query identity and diversity. The right part shows the structure of \ap , where red nodes are chosen as the MSA subset. To handle the vast repertoire of homologs within constrained computational budgets, the prevailing paradigm, exemplified by AlphaFold2 [ 19 ] and MSA Transformer [ 38 ] , relies on heuristic subsampling to fit sequences into narrow attention windows [ 42 , 49 ] . Whether through identity-based filtering via HHfilter [ 44 ] , clustering via MMseqs2 [ 45 ] or diversity-driven samplers such as divMax and divMin [ 38 , 49 ] , these methods typically reduce MSA subsampling to threshold-based filtering, clustering, or single-criterion selection under pairwise sequence-alignment metric [ 35 ] . While effective for removing near-duplicate sequences or biasing samples toward a desired diversity regime, they primarily operate on local similarity relations and do not explicitly model trade-offs w.r.t. diversity and query identities. This makes the explicit balancing of these two complementary aspects—query identity [ 5 , 44 ] and diversity [ 38 , 49 ] — a natural and worthwhile direction for MSA subsampling. Building upon this insight, we recast MSA subsampling as an explicit optimization problem rather than a heuristic sampling procedure, where biologically meaningful evolutionary measures such as diversity and query identity are elevated from heuristics to explicit objectives. This formulation allows subset assignments to be coordinated under carefully designed selection objectives. Leveraging this perspective, we propose \ap for MSA subsampling with Affinity Propagation (AP) [ 13 ] . Utilizing biologically designed unary factors and exemplar-consistency factors, AP’s exemplar-based message-passing reasoning provides a principled way to calibrate preferences within both sequence-diversity and query-identity aspects during MSA subsampling. To make this balance explicitly controllable, we further design two control knobs within the AP unary factor to regulate these two aspects respectively. The contributions of this work are threefold. First, to the best of our knowledge, we are the first to recast MSA subsampling from random or heuristic selection to an explicit optimization problem. Second, we introduce \ap , an Affinity-Propagation-based factor-graph approach that encodes the subsampling objective into factors and solves the resulting optimization problem through message-passing reasoning, with two control knobs in the unary factor to explicitly modulate sequence diversity and query identity. Third, evaluations on long-range contact prediction and conformational ensemble prediction show that \ap consistently outperforms baseline subsamplers, highlighting MSA factor-graph optimization reasoning as an important paradigm for downstream performance and validating the two control knobs as an effective design. 2 Related Work MSA subsampling. MSA-based PLMs reach state-of-the-art on structure-related tasks [ 19 , 1 ] , but the full MSA is rarely tractable: a query against UniRef or BFD [ 29 ] routinely returns tens of thousands of homologs while attention-based PLMs can only condition on a small fraction of them [ 38 , 19 ] , making subsampling a constitutive choice rather than an option. Existing strategies are simple heuristics applied only at inference on top of a pretrained backbone: random selection [ 6 ] ; HHfilter [ 44 ] by identity threshold; AFcluster [ 49 ] via DBSCAN for multiple conformation prediction, where biologically functional states can be recovered from MSAs; the greedy divmin and divmax [ 38 , 16 ] pushing each pick toward minimum or maximum diversity. How a subsampling strategy preserves an MSA’s evolutionary structure, and how that choice propagates to training, remains largely unstudied. We therefore propose a factor-graph approach that imposes evolutionary structure by reasoning over the candidate homolog pool rather than filtering it. Protein language models. Protein language models (PLMs) apply NLP-style sequence modeling to amino acids, learning residue-level representations that encode structural, functional, and evolutionary information [ 40 , 14 , 46 ] . Long-range residue contact prediction has become a key benchmark for evolutionary understanding, since sequence-distant but spatially close residue pairs can be recovered from co-evolutionary signal [ 37 ] . Single-sequence PLMs, including ESM-1b [ 40 ] , SeqVec [ 14 ] , and Tranception [ 32 ] , learn such signals implicitly and scale efficiently, but this implicit treatment often requires larger model scale [ 26 ] , can be less reliable for long-range dependencies and rare evolutionary variants without external context [ 19 ] , and provides limited direct control for downstream settings such as conformational ensemble generation [ 6 , 49 , 43 , 24 , 30 ] . In contrast, MSA-based PLMs such as MSA Transformer [ 38 ] treat the MSA as a two-dimensional sequence-by-position input, applying row and column attention to externalize family-level evolutionary signal in the alignment itself; this design is parameter-efficient for long-range contact prediction [ 38 ] . Related AlphaFold-family architectures, including Evoformer [ 19 ] and Pairformer [ 1 ] , further demonstrate the importance of MSA- or pair-conditioned representations for structure modeling. Factor graphs for joint reasoning. Factor graphs are a foundational probabilistic-modeling and optimization framework, going back to Pearl’s belief propagation [ 33 ] and the sum-product algorithm of Kschischang, Frey, and Loeliger [ 22 ] , with a modern variational view from Wainwright and Jordan [ 47 ] . They have driven progress across coding theory, statistics, computer vision [ 20 ] , and natural language processing [ 18 ] . The framework spans Conditional Random Fields [ 23 ] , undirected Markov random fields, directed Bayesian networks and HMMs, etc, all formulating inference as an energy-minimization or MAP optimization problem solved by message passing. Affinity propagation [ 13 ] is a classical method that jointly optimizes the selection of representatives from a candidate set, with unary and exemplar-consistency factors giving clean control over query identity and redundancy (rationale in Section 3 ). At inference, reasoning jointly over the pool, AP passes responsibility and availability messages on the factor graph until a stable set of exemplars emerges. 3 Method 3.1 MSA Subsampling: Background Multiple Sequence Alignments (MSAs) provide MSA-based protein language models with rich evolutionary context [ 38 , 19 ] . Let 𝐗 = ( x 0 , x 1 , … , x M − 1 ) ∈ ( 𝒜 ∪ { − } ) M × L \mathbf{X}=(x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{M-1})\in(\mathcal{A}\cup\{-\})^{M	imes L} denote an input MSA, where x 0 x_{0} is the primary query sequence, each aligned row x i x_{i} has length L L , 𝒜 \mathcal{A} denotes the vocabulary of amino acids and − - represents an alignment gap. In practice, the number of sequences in an MSA, M M , can be overwhelmingly large, often reaching thousands or more. This scale presents a computational bottleneck for the training of protein language models (PLMs). MSA-based PLMs such as MSA Transformer [ 38 ] process an input alignment as a 2D grid with R R rows and L L columns, and their axial self-attention scales as 𝒪 ​ ( R ​ L 2 + L ​ R 2 ) \mathcal{O}(RL^{2}+LR^{2}) per layer. Consequently, given a fixed token budget B B , length L L induces a maximum admissible budget of rows: N B ​ ( L ) = min ⁡ ( ⌊ B L ⌋ , M ) N_{B}(L)=\min\!\left(\left\lfloor rac{B}{L}ightfloor,Might) When M &gt; N B ​ ( L ) M&gt;N_{B}(L) , MSA must be subsampled to N B ​ ( L ) N_{B}(L) while strictly retaining the query sequence. Existing subsamplers provide simple mechanisms for fitting large MSAs into fixed token budgets. Random subsampling is cheap and budget-compliant, but it offers no explicit control over the evolutionary composition of the selected set. Heuristic alternatives such as HHfilter [ 44 ] and divMax [ 38 ] introduce more structured preferences, targeting dereplication or diversity promotion through query identity thresholds or greedy sequential choices. These methods are therefore best viewed 